st descriptive but operationally important for downstream clinical decision pathways. [Data: Relationships (5064)]\"},{\"summary\":\"MSI-H is closely related to defective MMR and also triggers Lynch syndrome testing recommendations in rectal cancer\",\"explanation\":\"MSI-H (High Microsatellite Instability) is described as a biomarker state related to defective DNA mismatch repair status. In colorectal cancers, MSI-H is one of the key results assessed as part of standard mismatch repair testing, and when MSI-H is identified in rectal cancer it suggests possible underlying hereditary cancer risk with recommendation for evaluation for an inherited cancer syndrome such as Lynch syndrome. [Data: Entities (2731); Relationships (5068)]\\n\\nThe community includes an explicit relationship stating that if rectal cancer has MSI-H, testing for inherited Lynch syndrome is recommended. This mirrors the dMMR-to-Lynch linkage and emphasizes that either result (dMMR or MSI-H) can serve as a decision point for hereditary syndrome workup. [Data: Relationships (5068)]\"},{\"summary\":\"The network frames results as opposing categories: dMMR vs pMMR\",\"explanation\":\"DMMR and pMMR are represented as opposite categories used to classify whether mismatch repair deficiency is present. DMMR refers to the presence of mismatch repair deficiency, while pMMR (Mismatch Repair Proficient) is used when mismatch repair deficiency is not present (i.e., intact functioning mismatch repair mechanisms). [Data: Entities (2730, 2732); Relationships (5065)]\\n\\nThis opposition clarifies the interpretive structure clinicians use: a tumor is placed into a binary MMR-status bucket (deficient vs proficient), which then affects what follow-up evaluations may be considered (e.g., hereditary testing when deficiency is present). The dataset explicitly encodes this contrast as a relationship between dMMR and pMMR. [Data: Relationships (5065)]\"},{\"summary\":\"Microsatellite status is also presented as opposing categories: MSI-H vs MSS\",\"explanation\":\"MSI-H is contrasted with MSS (Microsatellite Stable) as alternative microsatellite status categories. MSS is defined as the opposite category to MSI-H, indicating that MSI-H is not present. [Data: Entities (2731, 2733); Relationships (5069)]\\n\\nBy encoding MSI-H versus MSS as a direct contrast, the community shows a second binary classification axis that parallels MMR status. This improves interpretability of test outcomes by making clear what a “positive” (MSI-H) versus “negative” (MSS) microsatellite result signifies in categorization terms. [Data: Relationships (5069)]\"},{\"summary\":\"Mismatch repair status is treated as a biomarker feature used to guide treatment decisions\",\"explanation\":\"The BIOMARKER entity is defined as a measurable biological or tumor feature used to characterize colon cancer and improve understanding of disease, and it explicitly includes mismatch repair status (distinguishing dMMR/MSI-H from MSS/pMMR). This positions MMR/MSI results as clinically actionable biomarkers rather than purely descriptive findings. [Data: Entities (2678)]\\n\\nA relationship in the dataset further states that mismatch repair status is described as a biomarker feature that can guide treatment. This ties the testing framework to therapeutic decision-making and indicates the broader clinical utility of these categories beyond hereditary evaluation alone. [Data: Relationships (4938)]\"},{\"summary\":\"The community emphasizes a combined diagnostic pathway: biomarker testing informs both treatment planning and inherited syndrome evaluation\",\"explanation\":\"Across the entities, biomarker results (including dMMR and MSI-H) are described as guiding treatment decisions and directing further diagnostic steps, such as determining whether additional testing is warranted to evaluate for inherited cancer syndromes. This dual-use role is central to the community’s structure. [Data: Entities (2678, 2730, 2731)]\\n\\nThe r